   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "63e8b9a3-a507-4d5b-a79f-9c5bb473bd6f",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "Now that we have our predictions, we can perform some diagnostics. The first is accuracy, which is merely a percentage of the number of correct predictions."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": 35,
   "id": "001b0332-cf1d-404b-8da6-5c2ce3012b69",
   "metadata": {
    "tags": []
   },
   "outputs": [
    {
     "name": "stdout",
     "output_type": "stream",
     "text": [
      "Accuracy: 96.38%\n"
     ]
    }
   ],
   "source": [
    "accuracy = (np.sum(np.diag(confusion_matrix(y_test, y_pred)))) / (np.sum(confusion_matrix(y_test, y_pred))) \n",
    "print(f'Accuracy: {accuracy*100:.2f}%')"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "9f7c8d5c-c4ae-48d0-b2d0-8c24b6fa0cef",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "~92% is quite high! Let's try to decompose this score to see where the model hiccuped.\n",
    "\n",
    "Below is a \"classification report,\" detailing the \"precision,\" \"recall,\" and \"f1-score\" for each of the three Feature Groups (0, 1, 2). Concentrating on the columns for the moment, using Feature Group 2 (FC2) as an example,\n",
    "\n",
    "* Precision: when the model predicts FC2, how often is it correct?\n",
    "* Recall: how many of the k-means FC2 galaxies were correctly labeled as FC2? does it miss \"actual\" FC2 galaxies?\n",
    "* f1-score: what is the average precision and recall percentage?\n",
    "\n",
    "Looking at the table below, I immediately notice that FC2 is showing a high precision but with a considerably lower recall score: the model predicts FC2 galaxies correctly ~96% of the time but misses \"actual\" FC2 galaxies more often than for FC0 and FC1. That is, the model fails to capture a significant percentage of \"actual\" FC2 galaxies, thinking the galaxies belong instead to FC0 or FC1."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": 36,
   "id": "120f799d-d096-4a1c-9e30-91546df0654f",
   "metadata": {
    "tags": []
   },
   "outputs": [
    {
     "name": "stdout",
     "output_type": "stream",
     "text": [
      "              precision    recall  f1-score   support\n",
      "\n",
      "           0      0.954     0.981     0.967       481\n",
      "           1      0.970     0.925     0.947       213\n",
      "           2      0.978     0.963     0.970       272\n",
      "\n",
      "    accuracy                          0.964       966\n",
      "   macro avg      0.967     0.956     0.962       966\n",
      "weighted avg      0.964     0.964     0.964       966\n",
      "\n"
     ]
    }
   ],
   "source": [
    "print(classification_report(y_test, y_pred, digits=3))"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "d803d313-a54c-42aa-91f3-377a926378e8",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "The confusion matrix gives even more of a decomposition, showing the galaxies (mis)classified for each of the three Feature Groups. The rows are the \"actual\" Feature Groups for FC0 (top) to FC2 (bottom), while the columns are the model-predicted Feature Groups for FC0 (left) to FC2 (right). The diagonal integers are the number of galaxies correctly identified, and the off-diagonal elements indicate how many were misclassified and for what Feature Group. The 16, for instance, shows that 16 of \"actual\" FC2 galaxies were misclassified as FC0 galaxies.\n",
    "\n",
    "A notable number of FC2 galaxies, as seen below, were misclassified as either FC0 (16/110) or FC1 (8/110). Changing the random seed to vary the train/test sets and the model creation did affect the symmetry of those bottom two numbers -- sometimes more FC2 galaxies were misclassified as FC1, and sometimes more FC2 galaxies were misclassified as FC0 -- but the overall conclusion remained the same: \n",
    "\n",
    "Random Forest misclassified ~15-20% of the \"actual\" FC2 sample."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": 37,
   "id": "c43cb344-2072-4f52-b4ea-d55413d7810e",
   "metadata": {
    "tags": []
   },
   "outputs": [
    {
     "name": "stdout",
     "output_type": "stream",
     "text": [
      "[[472   5   4]\n",
      " [ 14 197   2]\n",
      " [  9   1 262]]\n"
     ]
    }
   ],
   "source": [
    "cm = confusion_matrix(y_test, y_pred, labels=[0,1,2])\n",
    "print(cm)"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "ace1fc48-5fc8-4b14-ab8d-a5df45025f89",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "Also of note is that the misclassified galaxies largely lie along the border between clusters in PCA space. The figure below is a *reimagined* version of the PCA-space plot of the Feature Clusters shown above. Now, however, it is explicitly showing the full sample (small colored points); the test sample that the model classified (larger points); and the galaxies classified by k-means as FC1 but misclassified as either FC0 (red open circles) or FC2 (blue open circles).\n",
    "\n",
    "For consistency purposes (again), I create a custom colormap mimicking the scheme above so I (and readers) can keep track of Feature Groups between all figures."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": 38,
   "id": "f0a1bf0d-0f04-4206-8033-ae33ef4a1d29",
   "metadata": {
    "tags": []
   },
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "#the special custom colormap...\n",
    "\n",
    "import matplotlib.colors as mcolors\n",
    "\n",
    "colors = ['darkorange','seagreen','deeppink']\n",
    "cmap = mcolors.ListedColormap(colors)"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": 39,
   "id": "787163d9-8b79-4329-8f30-da1eae5ffb93",
   "metadata": {
    "tags": []
   },
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "#grab the full dataframe row-matched to X_test and y_test\n",
    "df_test = df.loc[X_test.index].copy()\n",
    "\n",
    "#create two new bool columns incidating the actual FC2 galaxies which the ML model predicts to \n",
    "#  be FC0 (FC2_to_0) or (FC1 FC2_to_1)\n",
    "df_test['FC1_to_0'] = (y_test == 1) & (y_pred == 0)\n",
    "df_test['FC1_to_2'] = (y_test == 1) & (y_pred == 2)"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": 86,
   "id": "940833c6-baea-43d7-ad79-bb688682ef61",
   "metadata": {
    "tags": []
   },
   "outputs": [
    {
     "data": {
      "image/png": "",
      "text/plain": [
       "<Figure size 600x500 with 1 Axes>"
      ]
     },
     "metadata": {},
     "output_type": "display_data"
    }
   ],
   "source": [
    "#initialize the figure\n",
    "plt.figure(figsize=(6,5))\n",
    "\n",
    "#plot the background points -- all of the galaxy data in PCA space\n",
    "plt.scatter(df['Comp1'], df['Comp2'], c=df['Feature Cluster'], cmap=cmap, s=5, alpha=0.1)\n",
    "\n",
    "#plot the test sample that the model classified\n",
    "plt.scatter(df_test['Comp1'], df_test['Comp2'], c=y_test, cmap=cmap, alpha=0.3)\n",
    "\n",
    "#add the FC2 --> FC0 overlay (FC2 galaxies which were misclassified as FC0)\n",
    "plt.scatter(df_test['Comp1'][df_test['FC1_to_0']],\n",
    "            df_test['Comp2'][df_test['FC1_to_0']],\n",
    "            facecolors='none', edgecolors='red', s=80, label='FC1→0')\n",
    "\n",
    "#add the FC2 --> FC1 overlay (FC2 galaxies which were misclassified as FC1)\n",
    "plt.scatter(df_test['Comp1'][df_test['FC1_to_2']],\n",
    "            df_test['Comp2'][df_test['FC1_to_2']],\n",
    "            facecolors='none', edgecolors='blue', s=80, label='FC1→2')\n",
    "\n",
    "#add legend, axis labels, title.\n",
    "plt.legend()\n",
    "plt.xlabel('Component One')\n",
    "plt.ylabel('Component Two')\n",
    "plt.title('Feature Clusters in PCA Space')\n",
    "\n",
    "plt.show()"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "2fecc0cf-4f40-4275-8e19-8b8abbc2dc83",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "We can also take a moment to visually inspect the distribution of points in each Feature Group. Compared with FC0 (orange) especially, FC1 *and* FC2 are less compact with respect to both of the PCA axes. Is this finding quantifiable? Yes, in fact.\n",
    "\n",
    "One option is to calculate the average scatter of features relative to their mean, for all galaxies in each of the three Feature Groups (not only the test samples!). Here is some *pseudocode* to articulate the approach:\n",
    "\n",
    "* For every Feature Group,\n",
    "    * For every feature column in that Feature Group,\n",
    "        * Calculate the mean feature value (create a row of averages with length `len(features)`)\n",
    "    * For every galaxy in that Feature Group,\n",
    "        * Calculate the difference between that galaxy's features and the corresponding means\n",
    "        * Square the differences, yielding a Euclidean distance measurement from that galaxy to the k-means\n",
    "          cluster centroid (in the actual N-dimensional feature space, not PCA space!)\n",
    "        * Sum each of these terms, the final step in the equation $D^2 = (p_1 - q_1)^2 + (p_2 - q_2) + ...$\n",
    "    * With the distance squared terms for every galaxy row, calculate the average (this is the mean square radius; the squaring is meant to more strongly penalize larger distances)\n",
    "\n",
    "Once this block of code executes, we will have quantative representations of scatter to compare between the three Feature Groups! Note that the choice of Euclidean distance here is largely for...say it with me...internal consistency: since k-means clustering utilizes Euclidean distances, our scatter measures with utilize Euclidean distances."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": 87,
   "id": "9f84fe2e-119c-4efa-87a4-8448c1d6ad4a",
   "metadata": {
    "tags": []
   },
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "#convert to numpy arrays so the indices line up\n",
    "Xv = X.values\n",
    "yv = y.values\n",
    "\n",
    "scatter = {}\n",
    "\n",
    "for k in [0,1,2]:\n",
    "   \n",
    "\n",
    "    Xk = Xv[yv == k]       #isolates table rows belonging to Feature Cluster k\n",
    "                           #written as a numpy matrix. yay.\n",
    "                           #rows (axis=0) are galaxies, columns (axis=1) are features\n",
    "    \n",
    "    mu = Xk.mean(axis=0)   #calculates the mean for all feature columns -- creates array of length len(Features)\n",
    "                           #Xk.mean() calculates mean of ALL values in Xk\n",
    "                           #Xk.mean(axis=1) calculates mean for every galaxy row\n",
    "            \n",
    "    intercluster_distances = np.sum((Xk - mu)**2, axis=1) #axis=1 means that mu will be subtracted from\n",
    "                                                          #every galaxy row feature-wise. those individual \n",
    "                                                          #row feature values will then be squared, \n",
    "                                                          #and finally summed.\n",
    "                                                          #the result is one squared distance per galaxy.\n",
    "            \n",
    "    scatter[f'FC{k}'] = float(f'{np.mean(intercluster_distances):.3f}')   #find average scatter in features.\n",
    "                                                        #np.mean then calculates the mean squared distance for\n",
    "                                                        #all of the galaxies in the Feature Group."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": 88,
   "id": "aba85168-0492-441e-aeb0-5e3bf9a9ef1f",
   "metadata": {
    "tags": []
   },
   "outputs": [
    {
     "data": {
      "text/plain": [
       "{'FC0': 1.713, 'FC1': 3.637, 'FC2': 3.266}"
      ]
     },
     "execution_count": 88,
     "metadata": {},
     "output_type": "execute_result"
    }
   ],
   "source": [
    "scatter"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "f38c7035-7977-4667-a524-4ca3f6593cce",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "This finding reinforces what the raincloud plots convey: systematically higher scatter in FC2. Indeed, while the figure directly comparing the input features for each Feature Group showed nearly invisible 68% confidence intervals on the median, the raincloud plots indicate that there is typically more intrinsic spread in FC2 morphologies - especially in Sersic indices, size ratios, and colors (note that the latter two, for which the 68% confidence intervals were much larger, were not involved in the ML model). \n",
    "\n",
    "We do observe such trends as a lower median size ratio and higher [NUV-r] color among FC2 galaxies; however, the spread of size ratios is high and the peak of the [NUV-r] distribution is in line with those of FC1 and FC0: systematically higher scatter in FC2. \n",
    "\n",
    "As we found in the previous section, k-means clustering created a Feature Group (FC2) that happens to harbor the highest fraction of Virgo cluster galaxies and the lowest fraction of field/isolated galaxies of the Feature Groups. Galaxy evolution literature shows that galaxies in high density environments tend to undergo significant morphology transformations (e.g., morphology-density relation; Dressler+1980) due to various galaxy-galaxy and galaxy-environment mechanisms. This, coupled with the Virgo Cluster being relatively young, irregular, and low-mass, is broadly consistent with a diversity of galaxy shapes and sizes among our cluster galaxies...despite trending toward lower size ratios, redder [NUV-r], and more early-type Sersic indices. "
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "069d5e97-a086-4afc-bfb3-b79314c95b44",
   "metadata": {
    "jp-MarkdownHeadingCollapsed": true,
    "tags": []
   },
   "source": [
    "## Measuring the Robustness of K-means Feature Groups [k=2-10]\n",
    "\n",
    "### Up until now, we have favored k = 3 as a modeling choice. This section will explore whether some quantitative analysis also justifies the choice."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "a906095f-f1fd-4689-a0af-6f13b2242a0a",
   "metadata": {
    "jp-MarkdownHeadingCollapsed": true,
    "tags": []
   },
   "source": [
    "#### A. Random Forest Classification\n",
    "\n",
    "##### Just as with the k=3 case above, I perform k-means clustering for the k=2-10 cases and evaluate how well a consistent random forest classification algorithm can determine the Feature Group assignments. Using my trusty script, I can automate this process rather efficiently."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": 98,
   "id": "9ecb37e4-9bda-46de-b5ab-f186463ad1b9",
   "metadata": {
    "tags": []
   },
   "outputs": [
    {
     "name": "stdout",
     "output_type": "stream",
     "text": [
      "####################\n",
      "\n",
      "k = 2\n",
      "              precision    recall  f1-score   support\n",
      "\n",
      "           0      0.983     0.986     0.984       345\n",
      "           1      0.992     0.990     0.991       621\n",
      "\n",
      "    accuracy                          0.989       966\n",
      "   macro avg      0.987     0.988     0.988       966\n",
      "weighted avg      0.989     0.989     0.989       966\n",
      "\n",
      "\n",
      "[[340   5]\n",
      " [  6 615]]\n",
      "\n",
      "####################\n",
      "####################\n",
      "\n",
      "k = 3\n",
      "              precision    recall  f1-score   support\n",
      "\n",
      "           0      0.971     0.979     0.975       481\n",
      "           1      0.927     0.948     0.937       213\n",
      "           2      0.966     0.934     0.950       272\n",
      "\n",
      "    accuracy                          0.960       966\n",
      "   macro avg      0.955     0.954     0.954       966\n",
      "weighted avg      0.960     0.960     0.960       966\n",
